WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.525
We have followed biological information from DNA to RNA to protein, and seen how identifiers and cross-references hold the records together.

2
00:00:09.525 --> 00:00:15.250
Now we turn to the literature, because that is where the evidence behind a record lives.

3
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.350
A record mixes kinds of information.

4
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:27.600
A sequence may have been assembled from experimental reads, a domain assigned by a computation, a statement about function added by a curator.

5
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:34.825
The publications linked to the record are how you get back to the methods and the observations behind each of those.

6
00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:39.450
Our HBB example gives us a question to carry into this part.

7
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.100
A record tells us that a variant affects splicing.

8
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.900
How did anyone establish that?

9
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.225
Which cells, which measurement, which comparison?

10
00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:56.000
Which brings us to the literature, because every claim in every record we have looked at this morning came from a paper.

11
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.850
A word about why this section is here at all, rather than being left for the students to pick up.

12
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.975
Everything we have looked at this morning is an assertion by somebody.

13
00:01:05.975 --> 00:01:09.450
A gene has this name because a committee agreed it.

14
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.700
A transcript is the reference one because two groups compared their annotation.

15
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.675
A protein has this function because an experiment was done and a curator read the paper.

16
00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:25.275
Databases make those assertions easy to use and easy to forget.

17
00:01:25.275 --> 00:01:32.875
Being able to get from a record back to the paper behind it, quickly, is the difference between using a database and trusting one.

18
00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:41.600
A literature search starts with the concepts in the question, and logical operators decide how the sets of matching records are combined.

19
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.900
AND keeps the overlap.

20
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:51.100
HBB alone returns about two thousand nine hundred records, and the word review returns over five million.

21
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.350
Put them together with AND and you have two hundred and forty-one records that match both.

22
00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:03.375
Note that at this point review is just a word being searched for; it is not yet a promise that the papers are reviews.

23
00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:05.800
OR collects alternatives.

24
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:18.775
HBB gives two thousand nine hundred, the phrase beta-globin gives nearly nine thousand, and their union is about eleven thousand distinct records -- not the sum, because records matching both are counted once.

25
00:02:18.775 --> 00:02:24.900
PubMed indexes the abstracts; PubMed Central holds full text where the licence allows.

26
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:34.050
The distinction matters when you cannot read what you have found: a PubMed record always exists, a readable article does not always sit behind it.

27
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.475
The next question is where in the record a term has to match.

28
00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:45.950
An unrestricted PubMed search covers the citation information, and it does not search the full text of every article.

29
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.800
Field tags make that explicit.

30
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:58.775
HBB on its own gives two thousand eight hundred and ninety-nine records; HBB in the title-and-abstract field gives two thousand seven hundred and one.

31
00:02:58.775 --> 00:03:01.800
That tag limits where the word may occur.

32
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.025
It says nothing about the quality of the paper.

33
00:03:05.025 --> 00:03:08.650
For review the distinction is about the kind of article.

34
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:14.900
The plain word gives over five million, because PubMed expands it in several directions at once.

35
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:21.175
The publication-type tag selects records classified as reviews, and gives about four million.

36
00:03:21.175 --> 00:03:27.175
With both tags, our AND search falls from two hundred and forty-one to one hundred and forty-eight.

37
00:03:27.175 --> 00:03:31.550
The search box takes free text, and that is the start of the trouble.

38
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:39.900
A gene name in free text matches the gene, the protein, the disease named after it and any paper that mentions it once in a discussion.

39
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.525
Quotation marks ask for a phrase.

40
00:03:43.525 --> 00:03:51.175
Beta AND globin treats the two words as separate concepts: they may be adjacent, reversed, repeated, or pages apart.

41
00:03:51.175 --> 00:03:56.575
On the eleventh of November that search returned about twenty-five thousand records.

42
00:03:56.575 --> 00:04:04.375
Quoting them, beta globin, asks PubMed to keep the two words together in that order, and the result is a much smaller set.

43
00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:09.425
The coloured strips on the slide are schematic, not excerpts from real papers.

44
00:04:09.425 --> 00:04:12.650
There is a second effect that is easy to miss.

45
00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:19.975
PubMed expands many unquoted terms through automatic term mapping, adding subject headings and word variants.

46
00:04:19.975 --> 00:04:23.375
Quoting a phrase switches that off for those words.

47
00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:29.600
So quoting changes two things at once: adjacency, and how much PubMed adds on your behalf.

48
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:41.150
The filters on the left do the same job as the filters in UniProt — species, article type, date, availability of full text — and they are worth applying before reading rather than after.

49
00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:44.300
Two filters here do more than the rest together.

50
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:55.250
Species, which removes the model-organism literature when you want human work and the reverse when you do not; and review articles, which is the fastest way into a field you do not know.

51
00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:01.725
A review from the last two or three years will give you the vocabulary and the landmark papers in an afternoon.

52
00:05:01.725 --> 00:05:06.750
That is not a shortcut around reading; it is how you find out what to read.

53
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:15.200
Parentheses decide which operator is applied first, and PubMed reads Boolean operators from left to right when you leave them out.

54
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.975
Take HBB AND thalassemia OR beta globin.

55
00:05:18.975 --> 00:05:26.700
Read left to right, PubMed first combines HBB with thalassemia, then adds every beta-globin match through OR.

56
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:36.075
So every paper about beta-globin qualifies, whether or not it mentions thalassaemia, and the search returns about nine thousand four hundred records.

57
00:05:36.075 --> 00:05:45.100
Group it the other way, HBB AND, then thalassemia OR beta globin in brackets, and HBB becomes compulsory for every result.

58
00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:50.400
The count falls to just under twelve hundred -- about eighty-seven per cent fewer.

59
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.800
The lower pair on the slide makes the opposite point: moving the brackets can also make a search larger.

60
00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.750
Parentheses are not a way of narrowing.

61
00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.350
They are a way of saying what you meant.

62
00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:07.175
The result list is ordered by relevance, which is computed and not meant.

63
00:06:07.175 --> 00:06:13.525
Sort by date when you want the newest, and remember that the first page is not a judgement about quality.

64
00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:17.225
A wildcard stands for the end of a word.

65
00:06:17.225 --> 00:06:23.900
In PubMed an asterisk matches zero or more characters, and at least four letters have to come before it.

66
00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.625
This matters here because of spelling.

67
00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:32.225
Thalassemia and thalassaemia, thalassemic and thalassaemic: four forms, one stem.

68
00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:35.775
Thalass-star matches all of them, and the plurals too.

69
00:06:35.775 --> 00:06:44.125
Searching HBB with the quoted single word gave nine hundred and nineteen records; with the wildcard, nine hundred and forty-one.

70
00:06:44.125 --> 00:06:54.425
A modest gain, and there is a cost: a wildcard turns off automatic term mapping for that term, so you may lose the related concepts PubMed would otherwise have added.

71
00:06:54.425 --> 00:07:03.625
An individual record gives you the abstract, the identifiers — the PubMed ID, the DOI — and, usually, links to the data the paper deposited.

72
00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:10.375
Those links are the way back from the literature into the databases we spent the morning in, and they close the loop.

73
00:07:10.975 --> 00:07:14.850
Having a query is not the same as having something to read.

74
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:22.700
The wildcard search HBB AND thalass-star, with the Review article-type filter switched on, leaves fifty-seven records.

75
00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:27.850
That filter works on the metadata of the record, not on the content of the paper.

76
00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:32.750
A different purpose calls for a different article type or a different date range.

77
00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:40.225
And the same decision -- what kind of paper do I actually want -- applies in any literature database, not just this one.

78
00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:43.000
Note the identifiers as you go.

79
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:52.125
A PubMed ID is short, unambiguous and permanent; an author-and-year in your notes is none of those by the time you come back to them in six months.

80
00:07:52.125 --> 00:07:55.650
And keep the query itself, not only the results.

81
00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:03.575
A search is part of a method: "PubMed, this query, this date, this many hits" is reproducible, and "I searched PubMed" is not.

82
00:08:03.575 --> 00:08:06.850
The same is true of everything else this morning.

83
00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:11.550
Which resource, which release, which assembly, which accession with which version.

84
00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.875
None of it is difficult.

85
00:08:13.875 --> 00:08:17.475
All of it is forgotten by the time anybody asks.

86
00:08:18.075 --> 00:08:21.875
Now read the titles with the original question in mind.

87
00:08:21.875 --> 00:08:29.100
Jaing and colleagues, two thousand and twenty-one, a narrative review of the molecular genetics of beta-thalassaemia.

88
00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:36.150
The abstract ties the disease to HBB, which is what tells us the hit is relevant rather than merely matching.

89
00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:40.300
Now the part that makes search worth learning rather than merely using.

90
00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:44.150
A query with two words in it is a question about a set.

91
00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:51.300
AND gives you the records where both terms appear — the overlap, and usually far fewer records than people expect.

92
00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.900
OR gives you either, which is how you cover synonyms.

93
00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.100
These operators are not a PubMed feature.

94
00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:07.425
They are set operations, and the same three work in almost every search interface you will meet, including the sequence archives and the structure databases.

95
00:09:07.425 --> 00:09:09.975
Learning them once is worth it.

96
00:09:09.975 --> 00:09:19.325
Most people never do, and search by typing more words into the box and hoping, which widens the result set at exactly the moment they wanted it narrowed.

97
00:09:19.925 --> 00:09:27.175
The citation record gathers the title, the authors, the publication details and the abstract in one place.

98
00:09:27.175 --> 00:09:30.825
Underneath, PubMed lists the routes to the full text.

99
00:09:30.825 --> 00:09:39.150
For this review there is a free copy in PubMed Central; for other papers it may be the publisher's site, or your university library.

100
00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.850
A free copy is convenient.

101
00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:44.575
It is not a measure of relevance.

102
00:09:44.575 --> 00:09:46.350
AND narrows.

103
00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:52.150
Use it when both conditions must hold: a gene and a disease, a protein and a method.

104
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:59.700
And here is the article itself: the same title and authors, then the discussion, the figures and the reference list.

105
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:07.075
From here you can either read the part that answers your question, or follow the review's citations back to the studies it rests on.

106
00:10:07.075 --> 00:10:10.450
OR widens, and its real use is synonyms.

107
00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:18.800
A protein with three names in the literature needs all three in the query, joined by OR, or you are silently reading a third of the field.

108
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.700
The same citation record offers a second route onwards.

109
00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:29.750
Scroll down to the MeSH terms -- the subject headings assigned to the article by an indexer.

110
00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:36.925
One of them is beta-Thalassemia, slash, genetics: the disease, and the aspect of it that this paper discusses.

111
00:10:36.925 --> 00:10:42.800
The value of a controlled vocabulary is that it is the same whatever words the authors chose.

112
00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.300
The asterisk beside a heading marks it as a major topic of the paper.

113
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:54.875
One caution: a recently added record may not be indexed yet, so a MeSH search alone can miss the newest work.

114
00:10:54.875 --> 00:10:58.150
NOT excludes, and it is the dangerous one.

115
00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:05.925
Excluding a word removes every paper that mentions it anywhere, including the one paper that mentions it in order to rule it out.

116
00:11:05.925 --> 00:11:08.900
Use it last, and check what it removed.

117
00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:17.200
The safe way to use exclusion is to run the search without it first, look at what you would have thrown away, and only then add the NOT.

118
00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:25.725
It takes one extra search, and it prevents the failure where the paper that would have changed your mind was removed because of a word in its abstract.

119
00:11:26.325 --> 00:11:32.075
Selecting the heading and choosing Search in PubMed turns the subject heading into a query.

120
00:11:32.075 --> 00:11:41.275
The slash names the aspect, genetics, and the tag MAJR asks for papers where that subject is a major topic rather than mentioned in passing.

121
00:11:41.275 --> 00:11:44.950
Quotation marks turn a list of words into a phrase.

122
00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:55.850
Without them, a two-word term is two separate conditions, and the search returns anything containing both words anywhere — in different sentences, different sections, different meanings.

123
00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:59.500
With them, the words must appear together in that order.

124
00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:08.825
The generated query is beta-Thalassemia slash genetics, as a major topic, and the live page returns about two hundred results.

125
00:12:08.825 --> 00:12:11.475
Look at the top of that page, though.

126
00:12:11.475 --> 00:12:14.275
It says: filters applied, Review.

127
00:12:14.275 --> 00:12:19.475
The filter we set two searches ago is still on, and it is shaping this list too.

128
00:12:19.475 --> 00:12:26.325
That is worth a habit of its own -- when a result count surprises you, read the filters before you read the papers.

129
00:12:26.325 --> 00:12:28.675
The difference is not small.

130
00:12:28.675 --> 00:12:37.250
For a term like "beta globin" the unquoted search returns a large multiple of the quoted one, and almost all of the extra is noise.

131
00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:40.100
If a term has two words, quote it.

132
00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:44.675
So far we have gone from a question to a query to a paper.

133
00:12:44.675 --> 00:12:50.725
There is another way in, which is to put the uncertainty itself into words and ask an assistant.

134
00:12:50.725 --> 00:12:57.350
The question here is how a change inside an HBB intron could affect the RNA made from that gene.

135
00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:06.025
We will follow one conversation, making each request more specific than the last, until we have papers and findings we can check ourselves.

136
00:13:06.025 --> 00:13:09.500
The replies on these slides are shortened for the lecture.

137
00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.400
Four things to take away from the morning.

138
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:20.675
Decide what you are actually asking before you type anything, because the database you choose is a consequence of the question and not of habit.

139
00:13:20.675 --> 00:13:27.950
Use the official name, and the right identifier for the level you mean — gene, transcript or protein — with its version.

140
00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:35.200
Build the query deliberately: one organism, synonyms joined with OR, phrases quoted, exclusions last.

141
00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.550
And check what you have found against where it came from.

142
00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:46.525
Every number in these records has a study behind it, every annotation has an evidence code, and both are two clicks away.

143
00:13:46.525 --> 00:13:51.875
The lab this afternoon asks you to do precisely that for one protein of your own.

144
00:13:51.875 --> 00:13:55.725
A last word about the lab, so that the connection is clear.

145
00:13:55.725 --> 00:14:05.625
You will take one protein and build a dossier on it: the gene, the transcript, the protein, the structure if there is one, the domains, and the evidence behind the function.

146
00:14:05.625 --> 00:14:18.775
Every step uses something from this morning, and the awkward parts are deliberate — the protein with three names, the transcript with no agreed representative, the annotation that turns out to have been inferred rather than measured.

147
00:14:18.775 --> 00:14:21.425
The aim is not to finish quickly.

148
00:14:21.425 --> 00:14:25.900
It is to notice, each time, which of those things you are looking at.

149
00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.875
The opening question is deliberately broad.

150
00:14:29.875 --> 00:14:35.600
We know the gene and we know the process, but we have not chosen a variant or an experiment.

151
00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:38.725
Worth pausing on what would actually answer it.

152
00:14:38.725 --> 00:14:42.400
An explanation of splicing would give us a mechanism.

153
00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:45.000
A paper would give us a measurement.

154
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:49.825
Those are different things, and the first prompt asks for neither in particular.

155
00:14:50.425 --> 00:15:04.200
The reply describes a route from an intronic change to a biological consequence: a change in a splice signal can move where the RNA is cut and joined, part of an intron can be retained, and less functional beta-globin is made.

156
00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.175
That is a mechanism to go looking for.

157
00:15:07.175 --> 00:15:10.375
It is not evidence that anyone measured it.

158
00:15:10.375 --> 00:15:14.525
So the next question has to ask for something different in kind.

159
00:15:15.125 --> 00:15:22.300
Now we ask for two primary studies -- papers in which the authors report their own experiments or observations.

160
00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:27.875
Staying in the same conversation keeps the biological context we have already built.

161
00:15:27.875 --> 00:15:36.550
What has changed is the kind of answer we are asking for: not an explanation, but examples that name a study, a change and an observation.

162
00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:39.075
Two is a deliberate limit.

163
00:15:39.075 --> 00:15:41.550
It keeps the reading manageable.

164
00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:45.525
Two came back, and they are not the same experiment.

165
00:15:45.525 --> 00:15:53.675
Reimer and colleagues looked at how the variant IVS-110 changes the splicing of human HBB RNA in a cell model.

166
00:15:53.675 --> 00:16:04.550
Nualkaew and colleagues asked something else: whether an intervention could restore some correctly spliced RNA in cultured cells from patients carrying IVS2-654.

167
00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:07.300
Different variants, different settings.

168
00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:13.525
The broad question has brought us to related biology rather than to two attempts at the same measurement.

169
00:16:13.525 --> 00:16:15.775
We will follow Nualkaew.

170
00:16:16.375 --> 00:16:18.625
A title is a lead.

171
00:16:18.625 --> 00:16:29.450
What we want is enough detail to follow it efficiently, so the third request asks for three specific things: the PMID, what the researchers measured, and the figure that shows it.

172
00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:31.425
Each has a job.

173
00:16:31.425 --> 00:16:34.925
The identifier locates the record exactly.

174
00:16:34.925 --> 00:16:39.075
The measurement tells us what kind of observation the claim rests on.

175
00:16:39.075 --> 00:16:43.100
The figure gives us somewhere to start reading inside the paper.

176
00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.175
Together they turn a recommendation into something we can check.

177
00:16:47.775 --> 00:17:01.600
For Reimer, the answer points at long-read RNA sequencing, with figure seven: one panel identifying the alternative splice site the variant creates, the next comparing the splicing patterns of the normal and variant constructs.

178
00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:11.800
For Nualkaew, the measurement is correctly spliced HBB RNA, by RT-PCR, in cultured patient-derived cells, and the evidence is figure three, panels A and B.

179
00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:16.075
Notice how the answer became more specific as the request did.

180
00:17:16.075 --> 00:17:24.300
That is the same principle as refining a query: say what you need, and keep refining until the result is something you can investigate.

181
00:17:24.900 --> 00:17:28.625
Now the checking begins, and it is two separate checks.

182
00:17:28.625 --> 00:17:31.275
The first is: is this the paper?

183
00:17:31.275 --> 00:17:43.350
PMID thirty-one million one hundred and thirteen thousand nine hundred and ninety-six leads to a PubMed record, and we match its title, authors and year against the suggested citation.

184
00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:46.625
The abstract tells us whether it is relevant.

185
00:17:46.625 --> 00:17:49.725
The second check needs the paper itself.

186
00:17:49.725 --> 00:17:56.150
The free full text in PubMed Central gets us to the Results and to figure three, which the answer named.

187
00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:02.050
Finding the article and testing the claim are different achievements, and we have only finished the first.

188
00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:07.150
Figure three has four panels, and they do not all measure the same thing.

189
00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:12.400
A and B concern RNA splicing; C and D concern haemoglobin A.

190
00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:17.075
Our claim is about RNA processing, so we read A and B first.

191
00:18:17.075 --> 00:18:22.550
Panel A shows the products of reverse-transcribing the RNA and amplifying it.

192
00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:31.775
The shorter band, two hundred and eighty-four bases, is correctly spliced RNA; the longer one, three hundred and fifty-nine, is the aberrant product.

193
00:18:31.775 --> 00:18:43.450
Panel B gives the quantitative comparison against untreated and control cultures, and the Results report a mean of eight point two five per cent correctly spliced beta-globin RNA at day ten.

194
00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.200
That is a percentage of RNA.

195
00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.450
It is not the percentage of cells corrected.

196
00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:59.075
One representative lane in panel A is labelled twenty-nine per cent, which is exactly why the mean in panel B, and the Results text, are what you quote.

197
00:18:59.075 --> 00:19:06.250
Panels C and D measure haemoglobin A by chromatography and report about seven per cent at day fourteen.

198
00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:10.250
Related outcome, separate measurement, separate statement.

199
00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:13.700
The model matters as much as the number.

200
00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:23.950
These were cultured erythroid cells derived from patients carrying the IVS2-654 variant -- cells on their way to becoming red blood cells, in a dish.

201
00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:34.550
The intervention was an engineered U7 RNA delivered by a lentiviral vector, compared against untreated cells, a vector control and a scrambled-sequence control.

202
00:19:34.550 --> 00:19:38.900
Those controls are what let us attribute the change to the intervention.

203
00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:51.300
So the checked sentence reads: in cultured patient-derived erythroid cells carrying IVS2-654, an engineered U7 RNA partly restored correctly spliced HBB RNA.

204
00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:53.875
The experiment supports that.

205
00:19:53.875 --> 00:19:57.425
It does not support a claim about treating patients.

206
00:19:57.425 --> 00:20:02.650
The assistant found us the candidate; reading the paper is what fixed the wording.

207
00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:06.450
Both of those examples were real papers.

208
00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:11.600
Now step back to an earlier question: does the suggested paper exist at all?

209
00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:15.175
This case is documented rather than invented.

210
00:20:15.175 --> 00:20:33.400
Researchers published ChatGPT output generated on the twelfth of April two thousand and twenty-three, and its first reference was a two thousand and twenty-one review by Chen and colleagues on sunscreen and the prevention of skin cancer, with a journal, a volume, an issue, page numbers and a PubMed identifier.

211
00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:38.225
Every attribute that normally makes a reference easy to trace is present.

212
00:20:38.225 --> 00:20:40.200
That is the point.

213
00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:43.925
The appearance of precision is not evidence of it.

214
00:20:44.525 --> 00:20:46.425
So we test it.

215
00:20:46.425 --> 00:20:55.175
Three details straight out of the citation: the word sunscreen in the title, the first author Chen A C, and the year two thousand and twenty-one.

216
00:20:55.175 --> 00:20:58.125
That fielded search returns nothing.

217
00:20:58.125 --> 00:21:01.500
Be careful about what that does and does not show.

218
00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:11.575
A search returning nothing cannot prove that no such paper exists anywhere -- indexing can be incomplete, and a wrong detail in the citation can break retrieval by itself.

219
00:21:11.575 --> 00:21:20.200
What makes this case solid is that the authors who published it searched several resources independently and classified the reference as fabricated.

220
00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.225
The second check is sharper.

221
00:21:23.225 --> 00:21:25.900
The identifier does resolve.

222
00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:33.675
It opens a paper by Wang and colleagues about materials for enriching histidine-rich proteins, in a materials-science journal.

223
00:21:33.675 --> 00:21:38.825
Title, authors and journal all disagree with the sunscreen review in the answer.

224
00:21:38.825 --> 00:21:43.925
So a working link, by itself, tells you nothing about the sentence next to it.

225
00:21:43.925 --> 00:21:49.650
That is the inconsistency you can see for yourself, and it sits beside the published audit.

226
00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:53.925
We have now met the simplest failure: a paper that is not there.

227
00:21:53.925 --> 00:21:57.700
When the paper is there, the second check still has to happen.

228
00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.325
Why does this happen?

229
00:22:00.325 --> 00:22:05.775
A model can produce citation-shaped text without having retrieved any citation.

230
00:22:05.775 --> 00:22:09.300
The shape is learnable; the record is not in it.

231
00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.800
Some requests make it worse.

232
00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:18.400
Asking for exactly ten papers with complete bibliographic details demands more than can be verified.

233
00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:24.075
A question that assumes the evidence exists invites an answer built on that assumption.

234
00:22:24.075 --> 00:22:34.525
Even with retrieval, details from different publications can be combined: a real author list, a plausible title and a genuine identifier belonging to three different works.

235
00:22:34.525 --> 00:22:38.975
These are mechanisms, not a diagnosis of the case we just saw.

236
00:22:38.975 --> 00:22:44.150
An ordinary prompt can produce a bad reference, and a demanding one need not.

237
00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:48.025
Here are two requests about the same biology.

238
00:22:48.025 --> 00:22:55.050
The first asks for a fixed number of papers with exact identifiers, and gives no room to say anything is missing.

239
00:22:55.050 --> 00:23:05.150
The second makes verification part of the task: fewer papers are acceptable, direct links are required, and anything that could not be checked has to be named as such.

240
00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:12.025
Those links are what let us go back to the records and compare the title, the authors and the identifier ourselves.

241
00:23:12.025 --> 00:23:20.725
This is a teaching comparison, not a controlled experiment -- we are not claiming the first prompt causes fabrication and the second prevents it.

242
00:23:20.725 --> 00:23:25.450
Whatever the wording, the person using the citation still has to check it.

243
00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:29.900
The last case is the harder one, because nothing is missing.

244
00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:41.500
In a published evaluation, ChatGPT was asked on the eleventh of May two thousand and twenty-three to summarise a named paper on saline and albumin in patients with traumatic brain injury.

245
00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:46.150
The paper was not supplied and the model had no internet access.

246
00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:51.425
The summary said there was no significant difference in mortality between the two fluids.

247
00:23:51.425 --> 00:23:56.175
The paper exists, the citation is correct, and that sentence is wrong.

248
00:23:56.175 --> 00:24:07.650
The original study reported mortality at twenty-four months of thirty-three point two per cent with albumin against twenty point four per cent with saline, with a p value of nought point nought nought three.

249
00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:11.925
The finding had not been misunderstood; it had been removed.

250
00:24:11.925 --> 00:24:15.175
This is why the second check cannot be skipped.

251
00:24:15.175 --> 00:24:19.025
A correct reference and a correct claim are different things.

252
00:24:19.625 --> 00:24:25.800
We began with a plain question: where do we find information about something like human haemoglobin?

253
00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:28.200
It opened several doors.

254
00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:35.725
A gene, a transcript, a protein, a structure, an expression measurement -- related objects that answer different questions.

255
00:24:35.725 --> 00:24:39.375
Choosing what you need comes before choosing where to look.

256
00:24:39.375 --> 00:24:42.525
These resources are a connected network.

257
00:24:42.525 --> 00:24:48.800
Archives share records, other databases enrich them, and publications sit behind the claims.

258
00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:52.800
Names and interfaces change; the principles do not.

259
00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:58.050
Identify the object, establish what the record describes, follow its links.

260
00:24:58.050 --> 00:25:00.350
Names help you discover.

261
00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:04.550
Identifiers and versions let you say exactly what you used.

262
00:25:04.550 --> 00:25:10.375
And the last step is always the same question we have been asking all morning: what supports this?

263
00:25:10.375 --> 00:25:18.425
A search service, a query, a filter, an identifier, and the figure you read -- write them down, and your answer can be checked by somebody else.

